# Allowlist: the build context is exactly what `cargo build --release` and the
# runtime stage read. A denylist let every new top-level file or directory
# (docs, tests, website, tooling state) into `COPY . .`, so a docs-only change
# busted the planner layer and forced a cold release rebuild; an allowlist fails
# the other way — a new file the build needs is missing and the build breaks
# loudly, on the PR's `image-check` job.
*

# The workspace manifest and lock, plus the toolchain file the builder honours
# (the Dockerfile's `FROM rust:<channel>` must match it).
!Cargo.toml
!Cargo.lock
!rust-toolchain.toml
# `[patch.crates-io]` stub; a workspace member `cargo chef` must restore.
!third_party/
# The crate itself and what it embeds: `sqlx::migrate!` reads both migration
# directories at compile time.
!src/
!migrations/
!migrations-mysql/
# The runtime stage ships it as the reference config.
!config/example.yaml

# Key material must never enter the build context, even under an allowed
# directory: `cache-to: type=gha` exports the builder layers. Deny by
# extension, not by path, so the next key added anywhere is covered.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.jks
**/*.crt
**/*.der
**/*.pk8
**/*.asc
**/*.gpg
# SSH keys carry no extension, so match the conventional names too.
**/id_rsa*
**/id_ed25519*
**/id_ecdsa*
**/.env
**/.env.*
